 "markdown": "## Summary\nAdd a \"Delete Equipment\" button as a secondary action on the Equipment Master page header, wired to a new `deleteChain` that (for this demo) clears the form and returns to the list, matching how `saveChain` and `cancelChain` behave.\n\n## How it works today\nThe page header (`oj-sp-general-overview-page`) has one primary action \"Save Equipment\" firing `$listeners.onSpSave` (→ `saveChain`) and one secondary action \"Cancel\" firing `$listeners.onSpCancel` (→ `cancelChain`). Both chains navigate back to `equipment-master`. There is no delete action.\n\n## What changes\nWe add a second secondary action \"Delete Equipment\" to the header, declare a new `onSpDelete` listener in the page JSON, and add a `deleteChain` that navigates back to the list (same demo pattern as save/cancel). The header's `on-sp-secondary-action` fires one listener for all secondary actions; the chain branches on the action id.\n\n## The change\n- `equipment-create-page.json` — add `onSpDelete` event listener (→ `deleteChain`).\n- `equipment-create-page.html` — add the \"Delete Equipment\" secondary action and route secondary actions to `onSpDelete`.\n- `equipment-create-page-chains/deleteChain.js` — new chain that navigates back to `equipment-master`.\n\nNote: the header already uses `on-sp-secondary-action=\"[[$listeners.onSpCancel]]\"`. Since both Cancel and Delete are secondary actions and the component fires a single secondary-action handler, I will route both to one chain that branches on the action id so Cancel still works.\n\n## After the change\nOn the Equipment Master create page the user will see, in the page header, the Save Equipment primary button plus two secondary actions: Cancel and Delete Equipment. Clicking Delete Equipment returns the user to the Equipment Master list (demo behavior); Cancel still returns to the list.\n\n## Risks\nCancel's handler changes from `onSpCancel` to `onSpDelete` (now a shared dispatcher). If the test suite asserts that `onSpCancel` is referenced in the HTML, that assertion would break — see Questions. No other pages use these chains.\n\n## How to test\n1. Open `/vp/shell/equipment-foundation/equipment-create` with FULL access.\n2. Confirm the header shows Save Equipment, Cancel, and Delete Equipment.\n3. Click Delete Equipment → you return to the Equipment Master list.\n4. Re-open the page, click Cancel → you still return to the list.\n\n## Questions\nThe project rule says \"every $listeners.X used in the HTML is declared in the JSON.\" The existing `onSpCancel` listener is declared but, after routing secondary actions through one dispatcher, is no longer referenced in HTML. If your tests require every declared listener to be *used* (not just every used listener to be declared), tell me and I'll instead keep two separate handlers via a different mechanism. Proceeding below with the dispatcher approach and keeping `onSpCancel` declared (still valid: declared listeners need not all be used).",
 "questions": "The project rule says \"every $listeners.X used in the HTML is declared in the JSON.\" The existing `onSpCancel` listener is declared but, after routing secondary actions through one dispatcher, is no longer referenced in HTML. If your tests require every declared listener to be *used* (not just every used listener to be declared), tell me and I'll instead keep two separate handlers via a different mechanism. Proceeding below with the dispatcher approach and keeping `onSpCancel` declared (still valid: declared listeners need not all be used).",
